# bpy Patterns for execute_blender_code

Battle-tested snippets for the build passes. Rules that keep runs safe and
repeatable:

- **Self-contained chunks**: every `execute_blender_code` call re-imports
  (`import bpy, math, mathutils`) and redefines any helper it uses — do not
  assume state survives between calls.
- **Small chunks**: one component / one rig / one material batch per call.
  Verify with printed output or `get_object_info` after each chunk.
- **Own collection only**: create/delete only inside the run collection
  (named `meta.name`). Never wipe the scene, never touch user objects,
  never save or quit the file unless the user asked.
- **Idempotent by name**: object names = spec component ids; creating a
  component first removes any same-named leftover, so re-running a pass is
  always safe.

## Bootstrap (start of every build session)

```python
import bpy

RUN = "coffee_mug"  # meta.name

def run_coll():
    coll = bpy.data.collections.get(RUN)
    if not coll:
        coll = bpy.data.collections.new(RUN)
        bpy.context.scene.collection.children.link(coll)
    return coll

def adopt(obj):
    """Move obj into the run collection, replacing any same-named object."""
    old = bpy.data.objects.get(obj.name) if obj.name in bpy.data.objects else None
    for c in list(obj.users_collection):
        c.objects.unlink(obj)
    run_coll().objects.link(obj)
    return obj

def replace(name):
    """Delete a previous attempt at this component (idempotent rebuilds)."""
    obj = bpy.data.objects.get(name)
    if obj:
        bpy.data.objects.remove(obj, do_unlink=True)

print("objects in run:", [o.name for o in run_coll().objects])
```

`execute_blender_code` returns printed output — end chunks with a `print` of
what was made (names, dimensions) and check it before moving on.

## Primitives → named component

```python
replace("body")
bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_cylinder_add(vertices=48, radius=0.045, depth=0.11,
                                    location=(0, 0, 0.055))
body = bpy.context.active_object
body.name = "body"
adopt(body)
```

Origins per `pivotNote`: position the 3D cursor, then
`bpy.ops.object.origin_set(type='ORIGIN_CURSOR')` with the object active —
or model at the origin so no fix-up is needed (preferred).

Parenting without a transform jump:

```python
child.parent = parent
child.matrix_parent_inverse = parent.matrix_world.inverted()
```

## Modifiers (form pass)

```python
m = obj.modifiers.new("Solidify", 'SOLIDIFY'); m.thickness = 0.003
m = obj.modifiers.new("Bevel", 'BEVEL'); m.width = 0.002; m.segments = 3
m = obj.modifiers.new("Subsurf", 'SUBSURF'); m.levels = 2; m.render_levels = 2
```

Bevel/solidify widths are in world units — **apply non-uniform scale first**:

```python
bpy.context.view_layer.objects.active = obj
obj.select_set(True)
bpy.ops.object.transform_apply(location=False, rotation=False, scale=True)
```

## Hollowing a container (mugs, bowls, bottles, vases)

`primitive_cylinder_add` and friends are solid, capped shapes. A vessel needs
an open top and a real wall thickness, which means deleting the cap face
before solidifying — solidify alone on a still-capped mesh just thickens a
solid block, it doesn't create an opening:

```python
import bmesh

obj = bpy.data.objects["body"]
bm = bmesh.new()
bm.from_mesh(obj.data)
bm.faces.ensure_lookup_table()
# pick the face whose normal points where the opening should be (+Z here)
top = max((f for f in bm.faces if f.normal.z > 0.9),
          key=lambda f: f.calc_center_median().z)
bmesh.ops.delete(bm, geom=[top], context='FACES')
bm.to_mesh(obj.data)
bm.free()
obj.data.update()

sol = obj.modifiers.new("Solidify", 'SOLIDIFY')
sol.thickness = 0.003
sol.offset = -1  # shell grows inward from the original (outer) surface
```

`offset = -1` keeps the surface you already blocked out and proportioned as
the outer wall, adding the new geometry inward — that's almost always what
you want, since the blockout pass already sized the outside to match the
reference.

