## Lighting & world

**Light energy (Watts) is absolute, not relative to your subject.** A 100W
area light is calibrated for room-scale scenes; pointed at a 0.1m object from
half a meter away it's a floodlight at point-blank range and blows every
render to solid white. For the small objects this skill usually builds
(tens of centimeters), start in the **1-3W** range per light, not tens or
hundreds. Two corollaries that are easy to miss:

- **Lights add up.** Three lights that each look fine rendered alone can
  still overexpose in combination — after wiring the full rig, render once
  and check for blown highlights before moving on, don't assume the
  per-light numbers you picked are safe together.
- **World background strength stacks with every light too.** A "just a
  little ambient fill" world strength of even 0.3-0.5 can wash out all the
  directional shading a key light was giving you. Start around 0.1-0.15.

Treat the numbers below as a starting point to render-and-adjust from, not a
fixed recipe — exact values depend on your object's size and the render
engine's exposure/view-transform settings.

```python
import math

def add_light(name, kind, loc, energy, size=0.15, rot=(0, 0, 0)):
    replace(name)
    data = bpy.data.lights.new(name, type=kind)
    data.energy = energy
    if kind == 'AREA':
        data.size = size
    obj = bpy.data.objects.new(name, data)
    obj.location = loc
    obj.rotation_euler = rot
    adopt(obj)
    return obj

# three-point rig around a subject ~0.1m tall at origin (rescale positions to
# your subject; keep energies in this ballpark and verify by rendering)
add_light("key",  'AREA', (0.30, -0.30, 0.35), 1.4, size=0.15,
          rot=(math.radians(55), 0, math.radians(45)))
add_light("fill", 'AREA', (-0.35, -0.20, 0.20), 0.3, size=0.2,
          rot=(math.radians(70), 0, math.radians(-55)))
add_light("rim",  'AREA', (0.05, 0.35, 0.28), 0.5, size=0.1,
          rot=(math.radians(-60), 0, math.radians(170)))
```

HDRI world (Path A: download via Poly Haven tools first, then):

```python
world = bpy.context.scene.world or bpy.data.worlds.new("World")
bpy.context.scene.world = world
# worlds already have a node tree by default -- no need to set use_nodes
env = world.node_tree.nodes.new('ShaderNodeTexEnvironment')
env.image = bpy.data.images.load(r"/abs/path/studio.hdr")
bg = next(n for n in world.node_tree.nodes if n.type == 'BACKGROUND')
world.node_tree.links.new(env.outputs['Color'], bg.inputs['Color'])
```

Plain backdrop instead of pure black, when not using an HDRI (materials pass
onward — pure black behind the subject makes it harder to judge silhouette
and edge shading, and isn't what most reference photos look like):

```python
